    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The Kanawha Valley area around Big Chimney was originally inhabited by prehistoric indigenous peoples who built ancient mounds and fortifications.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/big_chimney_west_virginia"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Big Chimney developed as part of a regional salt industry that began in 1797 with the first commercial salt furnace.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/big_chimney_west_virginia"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Local salt production peaked in 1846, using abundant brine springs and coal to fuel evaporation furnaces for food preservation.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        22
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/big_chimney_west_virginia"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The local economy shifted toward coal mining by the early twentieth century, supported by the Kanawha and West Virginia Railroad.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/big_chimney_west_virginia"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "Big Chimney is now a small rural community characterized by single-family homes and a mix of residential and light commercial areas.",
